<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Periyar River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Periyar is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, flowing about 244 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. A great flood in 1341 is said to have silted up the old port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a> near its mouth and opened a new harbour at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>, which then grew into the region's main port. Its waters feed the Idukki and Mullaperiyar dams.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/periyar-river.jpg" alt="Periyar River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KannanVM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fish_net_over_Periyar_River_0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
